	'nuances' => array(
		'LATIN AMERICAN LEXICON, one form sitewide: computadora (never ordenador), celular (never móvil), automóvil/vehículo/auto (never coche), manejar a vehicle (noun “conducción” only in fixed terms: “conducción autónoma”), costo (never coste), video (never vídeo), falla (never fallo, a court ruling here): “el costo de una falla es alto”, confiable (never fiable), asesoría (never asesoramiento), operador (never operario), desempeño for people, assets and business results, eficiencia for a machine ratio (“eficiencia mecánica”, never “rendimiento mecánico”), viáticos for travel costs, paro for a stop (“paro de emergencia”), renta/rentar, aplicación (“app” only for a phone app), computadora portátil or laptop, llenar un formulario, hacer clic, “comprobada” for proven (never “contrastada”); “manufactura” names the function or sector, the verb stays “fabricar”.',
		'INDUSTRY TERMS as Latin American plant engineers write them, beyond the term list: field balancing “balanceo en campo”, machinist “maquinista”, driveshaft “eje cardán”, gear “engranaje”, mill roll “rodillo” and rolling mill “molino de laminación” (never cilindro, tren), belt “banda” (“transmisión por banda”), conveyor “transportador”, blower “soplador”, air handling unit “unidad manejadora de aire”, electric armature “armadura”, commercial vehicle “vehículo comercial”, implements “implementos”, wet clutch/brakes “embrague húmedo”/“frenos húmedos”, voltage “voltaje”, AC/DC “CA”/“CD” (“1,200 V CD”, “motor de CA”), measuring runs “corridas”, standard deviation “desviación estándar”, safety guarding “guardas de seguridad”, deburring “desbarbado”, and “grinding” in a mass-correction list “esmerilado”, never “rectificado”. Every “prueba”, “ensamble”, “balanceo” compound follows the term list in titles, chips and FAQ alike: “Sistemas de ensamble y prueba”.',
		'NUMBERS — HARD RULE, the plugin does not convert numbers for this language, so what you write ships: keep the English number format exactly, the Mexican, Central American and US Hispanic convention: decimal point, comma for thousands (“0.002 mm”, “15,000 RPM”, “1,200 V”). Copy every figure character for character: never a decimal comma or thousands point or space, never add a comma the source lacks (“1100 mm”, “2500 Nm” stay) or drop one it has; a source “0,6 MPa” stays as written. Percent follows the source with no space: “98%”, “-10%”, “&lt;20%”, “al 95%”. Put a space between number and unit symbol where the English omits it (“16A” is “16 A”, “0.002mm” “0.002 mm”), letters and case unchanged (“240v” is “240 v”); ranges keep the source hyphen (“5-25 mm”).',
		'OWNER RULES: every unit symbol and specification is copied EXACTLY as the English writes it: RPM stays RPM and rpm stays rpm (never “r/min”, never “revoluciones por minuto”), LPM, GPM, HP (never CV), psi, MPa, bar, Nm, kW, kVA, V, A, ″ and +/- stay. Never convert, never add an equivalent: “36″ x 72″ x 30″” stays in inches with no mm. “1 ph”/“3 ph” is “monofásica”/“trifásica”, a kW motor size is “potencia” (“Potencia del motor de CA”), “@” meaning at is “a”/“al”. People’s names never change. Untranslated: Ascential, Ascential Care, Ascentialytics, Burke Porter, Burke E. Porter, BEP Europe, Cimat, Kleinknecht, Titan, Galileo, Lismar, DATAMES, WinterPark, XL Machine, Bauer, Universal Balancing, ADAS Garage, every model code; the tagline “Impossible? Done.” stays English.',
		'DATES: spell every date out, lowercase month, with “de”, in every slot, even where the English abbreviates: “Sept. 22, 2026” is “22 de septiembre de 2026”. Days and months are lowercase; clock times take “p. m.” (“2:00 p. m. EST”). A spec maximum is “máx. 6 kVA” or “6 kVA como máximo”, never a trailing “6 kVA máximo”. With “hoy” use the simple preterite: “today announced” is “anunció hoy”, never “ha anunciado hoy”.',
		'STYLE: no superlative, “líder” or “único” the English does not state; “world’s first X” is “primer X del mundo”. A machine’s working “envelope” is “volumen de trabajo”, never “envolvente”. Avoid gerundio de posterioridad (“, detectando…” → “y detecta…”), “a nivel de”, “en base a” (write “con base en”) and “de cara a”.',
		'Sentence case everywhere, never English Title Case. The FIRST LETTER copies the English first letter, string by string: English capital or acronym ⇒ Spanish capital, even in a sentence-case card label (“Fluid power” tag is “Hidráulica y neumática”, “CNC grinding” “Rectificado CNC”, “Industrial balancing machine” “Balanceadora industrial”); English lowercase ⇒ Spanish lowercase, even a lone word (“balancers” is “balanceadoras”), but a standalone “eCommerce” is “Comercio electrónico”. A heading capital never survives inside a sentence: “una balanceadora de cartuchos”, “vicepresidente de ventas”. Days, months, nationalities and job titles are lowercase. A category quoted in a UI string keeps its capital: “Mostrar 8 subcategorías de Máquinas balanceadoras”.',
		'Never leave a brand or model code in front of a Spanish noun the way English stacks it: head noun first, code after it: “CMT-VSR High-Speed Core Balancing Machine” is “Balanceadora de cartuchos de alta velocidad CMT-VSR”. “<Brand> <Product> system for <domain> applications” takes one frame on every page: “Sistema Titan de prueba de bombas de engranajes para aplicaciones de hidráulica y neumática con movimiento rotativo”, never “Titan sistema…”. A product name keeps one number in title, H1, alt and FAQ: “Prueba de transmisión hidrostática (HST)” everywhere.',
		'Attach every adjective and participle to the noun it really qualifies and agree with THAT noun: “Ventajas de la calibración dinámica de turbocargadores”; “Multi-section gear pump test” is “Prueba de bombas de engranajes multisección”, never “Prueba multisección de…”; “Sistema de carga externo”, never “externa”. A trailing participle after coordinated nouns agrees with the last noun only: “supervisión de alertas y optimización de la programación basada en datos”. An acronym takes the gender of its head noun: “el MUT” (motor en prueba). Sibling headings keep one number and article: “Especificaciones de la máquina horizontal” / “… de la máquina vertical”.',
		'Choose the preposition by what the equipment is FOR: “Component Test Benches” is “Bancos de pruebas para componentes”; keep “de” when English puts the workpiece first (“Rotor Balancing Machine” is “balanceadora de rotores”), but “machine for X” is “para” in every slot (“Balanceadoras verticales para herramientas y portaherramientas”), and a family named by workpiece takes “para” + plural (“Crankshaft Systems” is “Sistemas para cigüeñales”). Name the page’s own product with the definite article: “Longitud máxima de la herramienta”. English “for” linking two nouns is “de”: “costs for MRO” is “costos de MRO”. Before a phone number “at” is “al”: “llámenos al”.',
		'When the SOURCE glosses an acronym, carry every letter of the gloss and keep the acronym; APM is “gestión del desempeño de activos”. Shorthand for ordinary words is translated: SME (subject-matter expert) is “experto en la materia”, PM “mantenimiento preventivo”. Industry codes (NVH, ADAS, ECM, PLC, HST, MRO) stay, but an activity code never stands bare with an article in a list: “balancing, functional testing, NVH” is “el balanceo, las pruebas funcionales, el análisis de NVH”.',
		'Use the Spanish collocation, not the English word: a machine “ocupa poco espacio” (never “huella reducida”) and “admite” a range of parts, but a range of needs is “responde a”; “Key features include A, B” is “Entre las características principales se encuentran A, B”; products are “fabricar”, solutions “ofrecer”, services “prestar/brindar”, needs “cubrir/atender”; seamless → “fluido” or “sin interrupciones” (never “sin fisuras”); integral to → “integrado en”; “available with X” stays optional: “disponibles con corrección automática”; “tolerances as tight as ±0.002 mm” is “tolerancias de hasta ±0.002 mm”, after a label colon bare “hasta” (“Tolerancias: hasta 0.002 mm”); “tests for proper assembly” is “verifica el ensamble correcto”; the verb “retrofit” is “modernizar”, only the noun stays “retrofit”. “latest/newest” + product is “de última generación” after the name.',
		'CONSISTENCY — one English term or label gets exactly ONE Spanish rendering everywhere, page and site, in every slot: if the body says “prueba”, the title cannot say “ensayo”; a repeated table row keeps every word; every member of a visible set (tabs, chips, spec rows) agrees. A bare adjective label (“Electrical”) is masculine singular in every slot: “Eléctrico”. A feature named in a bullet keeps that wording when the FAQ repeats it: “Capacidad de control” → “la capacidad de control”.',
		'PUNCTUATION: lowercase the first word after a colon unless it is a proper noun or a separate sentence, in titles too (“Bancos de pruebas para motores eléctricos: pruebas de precisión…”). Quotation marks are “…” (nested ‘…’), never «…» (the base list’s Spanish «…» is Spain’s) or straight quotes. Open questions and exclamations with ¿ and ¡. A headline or CTA ends exactly as the source does, also inside markup: “Service on <span>every continent.</span>” is “Servicio en <span>todos los continentes.</span>”. Use the single ellipsis character …',
		'HEADLINES keep a definite article the English opens with (“El catálogo de productos de Ascential T&M”) and restore one Spanish needs (“presenta un nuevo modelo de negocio por suscripción”). Appointments: “promotes X to” is “asciende a X a”, “names/appoints/announces X as” is “nombra a X + cargo” with no “como” (“nombra a Erin Dillard directora comercial”), “hires X as” is “contrata a X como”, “welcomes X as” “da la bienvenida a X como”; one headline is worded identically on every card. The company’s own division or products take “su”: “amplía su portafolio”.',
		'Never render an English consequence clause (“allowing us to…”) with a bare gerund: “lo que nos permite adaptarnos con rapidez”. Never use a clitic whose referent is later or unclear. A gerund never qualifies a noun or ends a sentence as a bare tail: “y seguimos sumando”, never “y sumando”.',
		'An English imperative inside a sentence is imperative-usted: “Abra un ticket de servicio y nuestro equipo se pondrá manos a la obra”. But a feature or service description in a plan table or bullet list is a noun phrase, never a command or a subjectless verb: “Perform periodic verification…, and review…” is “Verificación periódica… y revisión…”; “Remotely monitor your machine” is “Monitoreo remoto de su máquina”.',
		'Do not carry an English nominalisation across as “la + sustantivo + de”: “This requires the scheduling of service” is “Requiere programar una visita de servicio”. In a noun stack attach each modifier to its own noun: “advanced NDT technologies” is “tecnologías avanzadas de pruebas no destructivas”.',
		'Several products under one trailing English head noun: pluralise the head and repeat the preposition (“Chassis, hub & steerable dyno” is “Dinamómetros de chasis, de maza y para ruedas direccionales”). The head governs both modifiers: “Assembly & Test Systems” is “Sistemas de ensamble y prueba”, never “Ensamble y sistemas de prueba”; a modifier inside one member stays there: “Pruebas de sobrevelocidad y de eficiencia en rueda libre”.',
		'A styling tag wraps the same words as in the source; move it only as far as word order needs and never pad: “See how we bring it all <span>together</span>” is “Vea cómo lo integramos <span>todo</span>”. A brand span covers the brand only.',
		'JOB TITLES, one form per rank sitewide: Manager = gerente, General Manager = gerente general, Director = director, Managing Director = director general, Vice President = vicepresidente (Executive = ejecutivo, Senior = sénior), Chief X Officer = director de X, CEO stays CEO. Inflect a woman’s title (“vicepresidenta”, “directora”; “gerente” is invariable). “Global” follows the unit noun: “vicepresidente sénior de operaciones globales de manufactura”. A unit named by a noun takes bare “de” (“vicepresidente de ventas de Transporte”). A business unit keeps its capitals only as the division in a title or headline (“de la división Aeroespacial e Industrial”); as a filter or sector label it is sentence case: “Aeroespacial e industrial”. “Americas” is “las Américas”.',
		'LISTS: keep every member of a series in one grammatical class; close a “de”-list with a noun phrase. If two options read “Prioridad alta” and “Prioridad media”, the third is “Prioridad baja”. A one-word table value adds no noun: “Next” is “Siguiente”. In running prose every singular item takes its article (“se encuentran la magnetización de excitatrices, la alineación de dipolos y un sistema de medición”); only bullet and label lists drop articles, on all items. A trailing “with A, B and C” stays “con A, B y C”.',
		'y becomes e only before an i-/hi- sound (“metales e integración”); o becomes u only before o-/ho-. After a negative, “or” is “ni” on each item: “sin afectar la seguridad ni la producción” (“afectar” takes a direct object here, never “afectar a la seguridad”).',
		'“donde” takes only a place as antecedent; after an abstract noun write “en los que/en las que”: “los problemas más exigentes, en los que el costo de una falla es alto”. Do not repeat a noun the English does not repeat: “What tests does the HST Test perform?” is “¿Qué verificaciones realiza la prueba de transmisión hidrostática (HST)?”; never stack a word on its own root (“desplazamiento de las fases y del resolver”).',
		'Dissolve heavy English passives: “Trusted by manufacturers who…” is “Nos eligen fabricantes que…”; never a pasiva refleja with a “por” agent: “El soporte lo brindan nuestros mejores técnicos”. Never mirror the English progressive with “estar + gerundio”. “Whether A or B” with verbs is “Ya sea que + subjuntivo … o …” (“Ya sea que diseñemos una solución desde cero o que optimicemos una bajo presión”); with a noun list “Ya se trate de A, B, C o D”.',
		'English -ing labels are not gerunds: a UI label, placeholder or heading takes the infinitive or a noun (“Searching…” is “Buscar…”); only a progress status keeps “Cargando…”. Follow Spanish UI word order: “New Chat” is “Nuevo chat”.',
		'Preserve the intensity and sense of English verbs: “ever-evolving needs” is “necesidades en constante evolución”; “one of our key strengths” is “uno de nuestros principales puntos fuertes”; “we engineer” machines is “diseñamos y fabricamos”, with an abstract object “desarrollamos”; “collect, mine and measure data” keeps three steps. “elevate/enhance” is a gain in quality, never quantity and never “elevar” with an abstract object: “Elevating support, Enhancing value” is “Mejor soporte, mayor valor”; “elevated support” is “un soporte de mayor nivel”.',
		'Never coin a verb from an English stem (“ingenierizar”) or calque a verb frame: “avanzar” is intransitive (“hacer avanzar”), “impactar” becomes “incidir en”, “empower X with Y” is “dotar a X de Y” (never “empoderar”). “impartir” only with cursos or capacitación.',
		'False friends: consistent → uniforme/constante (never “consistente”); actionable → útil para la toma de decisiones (never “accionable”); harsh → exigente; actually → en realidad; assist → ayudar (never “asistir”); eventually → con el tiempo; end-to-end → integral; insights → conclusiones; uncompromising → sin concesiones; self-service training → autoaprendizaje. world-class → “de clase mundial”, established Latin American business usage.',
		'Translate a word by its industrial sense. “location” or “facility” meaning a company site is “centro” (“Ascential facility” is “Centro de Ascential”; “56 service locations across 18 facilities and 38 field agents” is “56 puntos de servicio: 18 centros y 38 técnicos de campo”), never “instalación”; “planta” only for plant; “ubicación” for a physical position and the nav label “Ubicaciones”. On a service form “asset” is the machine: “Número de serie del equipo”. “Powertrain” naming a business area is “área de tren motriz”. An affiliate list is “Empresas afiliadas”.',
		'A lone nav fragment keeps its chrome sense: “Hub” is “Centro de recursos” (never “Maza” or bare “Centro”). A lone “Core” stays “Core” only as the Ascential Care plan tier (beside “Plus”, “Elite”); in a topic list or any turbocharger or balancing context it is “Cartucho”, never “Núcleo”. A lone “Garage” is the podcast ADAS Garage and stays “Garage”. A link keeps the noun naming its destination: “See all press” is “Vea todos los comunicados”; “Privacy” is “Política de privacidad”, “Cookies” “Política de cookies”.',
		'Open-ended enumerations take a Spanish formula, never a colon, with the openness marked ONCE: “including A, B and C” is “como A, B y C” or “incluidos A, B y C”, never adding “entre otros”; a string ending on “including” ends on “, entre ellos/ellas” agreeing with its head (“press releases including” is “comunicados de prensa, entre ellos”); “A, B, C and more” is “A, B, C y mucho más”. “most recently on <date>” is “el más reciente, el <fecha>”.',
		'“we help X do Y” is “Ayudamos a X a hacer Y”. “Trusted by X+ companies” is “Más de X empresas confían en nosotros”. Coordinate two verbs on one object only if each takes it directly; otherwise resume with a pronoun: “deliver and support intelligent systems” is “suministramos sistemas inteligentes y les brindamos soporte”.',
		'Visible Spanish never keeps “&”: write “y”, also across split strings (“Service &” + “Support” are “Servicio y” + “soporte”); keep “&” only inside a brand or division name (“Test & Measurement”, “Ascential Medical & Life Sciences”). A label “Category - Subtype” takes a colon, identically on every member (“Horizontal Manual - Wheel Sets” is “Horizontal manual: juegos de ruedas”); a “ - Imagen 1” suffix and a “ - Ascential…” title lockup keep their dash.',
		'No spaced em dash as a sentence break: write a colon, comma or period (“mucho más que una plataforma: es una promesa”); paired rayas only for an insert closed on both sides. A press dateline is “Shelby Township, Michigan, 22 de septiembre de 2026. Ascential…” (“Mich.” is “Michigan”, the Latin American press form). The boilerplate is one fixed string on every card: “Ascential Technologies, proveedor líder mundial de sistemas y servicios automatizados de diagnóstico, inspección, ensamble y prueba en una amplia…”, never “, un proveedor líder”.',
		'CAREERS addresses the candidate with usted, standard in Latin American corporate recruiting: “so that you can reach your full potential” is “para que pueda alcanzar todo su potencial”, “Are you ready to rise to the challenge?” “¿Está listo para asumir el reto?”. HR terms: benefits “beneficios”, compensation “compensación”, paid time off “tiempo libre pagado”, application “postulación”, “equal opportunity employer” “empleador que ofrece igualdad de oportunidades”. Employee testimonials keep the first person singular.',
	),
	'avoid' => array(
		'Peninsular forms: vosotros/vuestro, ordenador, móvil, coche, vale, coste, vídeo, fiable, monitorización, presupuesto (quote), nota de prensa, recambios, formación (training), asesoramiento, operario, in situ, turismo, par (torque), grupo motopropulsor, equilibrado, ensayo, mecanizado, utillaje, montaje (assembly), contador (meter), “afectar a” + thing, “ha anunciado hoy”, leísmo (“Le invitamos”).',
		'Country slang and colloquialisms: chido, padre, chévere, bacán, ahorita, platicar, checar, jalar, agarrar; never the verb “coger”.',
